{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head 753f6067002607d48cea5409bed90164158121f2 on builder/ar9-index-guards-20261117, all 3 artifacts, for request 972fa043 under my promise 56336e92. The implementation is AR-9 (request 99e7dd62, promise 941cad72). The reporting artifact is the AR-9 note. The head's parent is main f1322b1b.\n\nAR-9 closes the limit I recorded in the AR-8 approval (b389835e): the insert-only check read SQL text only.\n\nWhat I read:\n\n- The note first, then the whole diff.\n- `scripts/known-scope-insert-only.test.mjs`. `indexClassBody` cuts the `ScopeIndex` class from `packages/scope/src/worker.ts`, from its declaration to the first closing brace in column one. The test fails when the class is missing or when the cut body does not reach `this.ctx.storage.sql`, so a moved or reformatted class cannot pass empty. `deletesStorage` matches `storage.delete(` and `storage.deleteAll(`, not `deleteAlarm()`. `parseWranglerJsonc` strips comments and trailing commas outside strings. `indexMigrationRemovals` walks every `migrations` array, top level or under any `env`, and reports `deleted_classes` naming `ScopeIndex` and `renamed_classes` or `transferred_classes` moving a class from it. The tree test lists tracked `wrangler*.json`, `.jsonc` and `.toml` files with `git ls-files`, fails on any TOML file, and fails if `apps/room/wrangler.grow.jsonc` is missing from the list.\n- `threat-model.md`. The AR-8 paragraph names both guards.\n\nRequest conditions (99e7dd62):\n\n1. The check fails on a storage delete in the `ScopeIndex` class and on a tracked migration that deletes or renames it: met. It also catches a transfer.\n2. A named red for each form: met inside the test. The storage case adds a `deleteAll()` method to the real class body and finds it. The migration case finds the delete, rename and transfer steps in a JSONC sample, and it passes over a create step and a rename into `ScopeIndex`.\n3. One threat-model sentence: met.\n4. No runtime change: met. The head changes one test, one doc and one note.\n5. A note under `notes/implementation`: met.\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- The test. `node --test scripts/known-scope-insert-only.test.mjs` passes 6 of 6 at the head. That was the one light probe I ran. The tree has 15 tracked wrangler files, none in TOML, and the `ScopeIndex` class has no storage delete.\n- The `ScopeIndex` object hands helpers only `this.ctx.storage.sql` and `transactionSync`, never the storage object (`worker.ts:137`, `:140`, `:152`). A delete through a helper would therefore be SQL, which the AR-8 matcher covers.\n- The Node stage. `artroom-evidence/2026-11-17-ar9-node/node.txt` passes 242 of 242. The request allows the Node stage in place of a full gate for a scripts and docs head.\n- Scope. The 3 artifacts name the head, and their paths equal `git diff --no-renames --name-only f1322b1b 753f6067`.\n- Cheap checks. The commit-message check passes: 1 checked, 0 flagged. `check-notes` gives 0 warnings, and `git diff --check` is clean.\n- Wake rate. The head adds no runtime code.\n\nThe rate section answers the planner's question in 0859c7d3. ScopeIndex fell from 382 to 133 requests in 15 minutes, and every other object held its rate. The note attributes about 7 of the remaining 9 a minute to new object instances: each one asks twice for a birth by name, once with the kind and once without, and the cache is keyed by binding object. It labels this an inference. The two follow-ups it names are optional and are not part of this head.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The guards live in the same check as the AR-8 SQL guard, so one file states every way the index can lose rows.\n\nSecurity: no blocking finding. With AR-9, every removal path I named in b389835e now fails a check: SQL deletes, a storage delete in the class, and a class deletion, rename or transfer. If the second follow-up becomes a lane and the cache is keyed by index name, its review must confirm that these guards still cover every index the new key can